<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bluefields</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bluefields is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, at the mouth of the Escondido River. Named after a 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> pirate, it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a> kings under British protection and then of the self-governing Mosquito Reserve created in 1860. Nicaraguan troops occupied it in 1894, and it is now the capital of the South Caribbean Coast Autonomous Region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bluefields.jpg" alt="Bluefields" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ridiculopathy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historical_Museum_of_Afro-descendants,_Bluefields,_Nicaragua_09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bluefields lies on the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a>. The city stands beside the bay of the same name and consists of 17 neighborhoods, together with the port of Bluefields Bluff, which lies on a peninsula about 8 km away. Because of gradual erosion the peninsula is becoming a true island that closes the Bay of Bluefields on the east side. The climate is a trade-wind tropical rainforest one, with a drier period from February to April and extremely heavy rain for the rest of the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the early seventeenth century European privateers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, particularly those associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English interests, frequented the Mosquitian Shore and used the Bluefields River and nearby bays as harbours and meeting points. The area was already inhabited by several Indigenous nations, including the Kukra, Ulwa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a>. An early description appears in the Minutes of a Committee for Providence Island of 14 June 1637, in which Albertus Blauvelt, mate of the Expectation, reported a good harbour at the river's mouth. The British navy traded here and used the port for naval operations during the Anglo-Spanish War of 1779–1783.</p><p>Bluefields rose to regional prominence in 1844, when it was designated the administrative centre of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquitia</a>, which kept its independence and later a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> status under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a> was established in 1848 and founded the first organised schools in the region; among its first pupils was George Augustus Frederic, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a> king. Between the 1860s and 1880s British and American investment made the town a commercial hub for bananas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and maritime trade, with a population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a>, Miskito, European settlers and other Indigenous communities. Growth slowed after the Nicaraguan occupation of Mosquitia in 1894, when the city became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zelaya-department">Zelaya Department</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bluefields is the capital of the South Caribbean Coast Autonomous Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. The port was mined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1984 during the Contra War, and the city suffered extensive damage from Hurricane Joan in 1988 before it was rebuilt. It remains a centre of Afro-Caribbean and Indigenous identity and reflects the longstanding traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquitia</a>. It has two universities, a campus of URACCAN and the Bluefields Indian and Caribbean University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bluefields” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Britain maintained the Mosquito Shore settlement from 1749 to 1787 and later declared a protectorate, with <b>Bluefields</b> as the centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/mosquito-kingdom">Mosquito Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Managua of 1860 recognised Nicaraguan sovereignty while creating a self-governing reserve for the Miskito people, with its seat at <b>Bluefields</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mosquito-reserve">Mosquito Reserve</a></li></ul></section>
